Write down what collapsing the config files would actually cost

> The jekyll related things might be easiest to deal with if we put them in a
> `site/config/` folder and make jekyll ignore it. (e.g., we don't need
> ruby-version sitting around in root in this new paradigm)

> I forgot during my notes that Cloudflare was git connected. I can live with a
> little bit, but I'd like to find a way to collapse some configs.

I built the `site/config/` version to find out what it costs, then took it apart
again — because one of the four files in it is expensive in a way that is easy to
walk into by accident, and the findings are worth more than the move.

**No config moved in this PR.** It is one section in `docs/deploying.md`.

## The expensive one

**Jekyll looks for `_config.yml` in the directory it is run from, *before* it has
read any `source:` setting.** Measured with the file at
`site/config/_config.yml`:

- `bundle exec jekyll build` prints **`Configuration file: none`**, takes the
  *repository root* as the source, warns that the `default` layout is missing,
  and dies on a Liquid exception in `site/watch/archive.md`. It does not fail
  fast and it never mentions the config.
- `bundle exec jekyll build --config site/config/_config.yml` works, and the
  output is **byte-for-byte identical** to the build from the root.

So it costs one flag in four places — and the fourth is the **Cloudflare
dashboard build command**, the git-connected one that publishes the live site and
that no agent can read or set. Which is the thing you'd just remembered. Getting
it wrong does not fail the build; it publishes a broken site and reports success.

My recommendation: **leave it.** If that field is ever being edited for another
reason, take the move with it.

## The one that looked free and probably isn't

`.bundle/config` holding `BUNDLE_GEMFILE: "site/config/Gemfile"` is read by
bundler automatically, so `bundle exec jekyll build` keeps working with no
Gemfile at the root and **no command change at all**. Verified locally.

The part I cannot verify is the part that matters: nothing in this repository runs
`bundle install`. The live build works because Cloudflare's image detects a Ruby
project and installs gems itself. If that detection keys on a Gemfile at the root
directory, moving it means no gems — failing at the host while passing
everywhere else. And it trades one visible file for one hidden directory, so
there is barely a tidiness gain to weigh against that.

## The collapse actually worth having, and it is the one you named

`.ruby-version` and `.tool-versions` pin the same version, and **`smoke.yml`
carries a step whose entire job is checking that they agree.** Collapsing them
removes a file *and* a CI step — the only one of these that makes the repository
simpler rather than rearranged. `ruby/setup-ruby` takes either file by name, so
CI does not care which survives.

**Keep `.tool-versions`, drop `.ruby-version`.** asdf reads `.tool-versions`
natively, nothing needs per-user configuration, and it is your *"we don't need
ruby-version sitting around in root"* exactly.

**It is blocked on one fact I cannot check from here: does Cloudflare's build
image read `.tool-versions`?** Their image is asdf-based so it very likely does,
but "very likely" is not good enough for the file that decides which Ruby builds
the public site. One look at their docs or the dashboard settles it, and then it
is a five-minute PR.

The other direction — keep `.ruby-version`, drop `.tool-versions` — is safe at
the host and breaks local development quietly: asdf only reads `.ruby-version`
when `legacy_version_file = yes` is set in `~/.asdfrc`, which is per-user and off
by default, so a collaborator at the repository root silently gets whatever
global Ruby they have. That is the exact failure the `smoke.yml` check was
written to catch, reintroduced one level down. I would not take it.

## Also in the section

A table of all six root config files, what reads each, and whether it can move —
including `wrangler.jsonc` (no, wrangler searches from the directory it runs in)
and `advocate.yml` (untested; the engine may or may not care).

One measured detail kept because it will bite somebody: **`site/config/` has to
be excluded or it publishes.** The first build after the Gemfile moved put
`_site/config/Gemfile` and `_site/config/Gemfile.lock` on the public site. You
predicted that — *"and make jekyll ignore it"* — and it is worth having the
receipt in the file.
